think The living edge was ALES238 (Lotus catalog_verify). The named next Lotus crux — a signed carry naming who made a record — is a crypto/custody seam, so the Lindy-first crux-first move is not one more Lotus lap but opening Season G (Cryptography), the highest-Lindy new thread in the decision wave: it serves every module that will ever sign or verify.
think Of Monocypher's primitives (Ed25519, X25519, BLAKE2b, ChaCha/Poly), BLAKE2b is the crux-first opening — Monocypher's own hash, the foundation the whole signing stack stands on, and pure 64-bit arithmetic (no field math), so it is genuinely solvable in authored Rye this round rather than a season of its own.
think vendor/monocypher is an empty submodule — its network fetch is held on Keaton's hand (diagnosis 20260726.025926). So the honest, runnable parity ground is the RFC 7693 Appendix A known-answer plus Zig's independent std.crypto BLAKE2b; Monocypher passes the same RFC vectors and is added as a third target in a later rung without changing a byte. Named Monocypher-source parity as a horizon, not claimed.
think Authored the primitive from the algorithm — IV, SIGMA schedule, the G mixing function (comptime-unrolled, index bounds proven at compile time), twelve-round compression, byte counter and final-block flag, wrapping +% for the mod-2^64 adds. The primitive calls no std.crypto; std is used only inside the selftest as an independent oracle.
obs Self-approved the design round (active-designing/20260815-180728) rather than checking in — the crypto library is engineering; only signing with the maintainer's identity key is the gate, which this hash primitive never touches.
obs Named the rung semantically (Season G · blake2b), not a bare-letter ladder rung — the waymark for Season G is a later small round under the ladder law, and CION favors semantic labels regardless.
obs Constant-time timing-safety kept a separate named horizon — it wants measurement, not a claim; BLAKE2b's data flow is data-independent by construction.
